<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Self-Reflection</title>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <p>
        What do you think is your biggest knowledge gap right now?
        (Concepts/Types of problems)
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        What is your biggest strategy gap right now?
        What aspect of your problem-solving process isn't 100% solid, feels like
        guesswork, causes you to get stuck or make mistakes?
        Some examples: drawing a good diagram and identifying all relevant
        variables, uncovering physical assumptions, converting physics to math,
        sanity checks, <ellipsis/>
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Which of these do you think is limiting you more right now <mdash/>
        understanding of physics or faults in the problem-solving process?
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Don't just focus on the negatives.
        What are you doing well?
        How have you grown in the past few weeks?
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Do you have a study group?
        How do you get help when you struggle with something?
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        How much help would you say you needed with the past few assignments?
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Identify one specific, actionable decision you could take this week to
        improve your understanding of the course topics.
        An <q>actionable</q> decision means it should be specific enough to be
        clear if you are succeeding at following it.
        <q>Study better</q> or <q>Be more careful about X</q> is vague.
        <q>Find 4 problems online with a nontrivial-looking constraint and practice writing it mathematically</q>
        <mdash/> this is actionable.
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Is there anything you want additional feedback on?
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Is there anything you'd like to share with me about the course, or about
        how you're doing?
      </p>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>